ence we go carry on it is  a complete nonsense the whole thing it's  cruel it's barbaric and Frank to evil  yeah let's talk a little bit about why  you were there Dr Clark you specialize  in palliative care as I was saying how  are you seeking to help people in  Ukraine  well there's an enormous need at the  moment for for obvious reasons for good  end of life care but even before the War  Began eight months ago there was there's  precious little in the way of any kind  of palliative care infrastructure so I  came out with Henry at the invitation of  of some local political Care Specialists  to try and teach and train and support  doctors and also medical students in  delivering this desperately needed care  and one of the things that's happening  since the conflict began is hospices and  there are only 10 State hospices across  the whole of Ukraine not only are they  caring for the patients who are dying of  diseases like cancer but now casualties  from the front lines in the East are  being shipped over to hospices so now  they're looking after patients for  instance who maybe are a young soldier  who has been bombed maybe half their  brain has been being obliterated by one  of Putin's missiles and the people in  those hospices they're not used to  caring for trauma victims but they're  having to just roll up their sleeves and  do the best they can so any support that  we can provide and palliative care was  invented in the UK it's it the NHS is  the world leader in palliative medicine  it's an incredible opportunity to just  try and help practically and Dr Marsh I  was in Ukraine a few weeks ago and met  some British surgeons who were teaching  or helping to teach Ukrainian surgeons  particularly about trauma injuries  um give us a sense of the work that you  are able to do in terms of neurosurgery  well I'm I'm not a trauma surgeon and  neurosurgeon in fact is a very small  part of War Zone surgery because they've  been going to Ukraine for 30 years I  have dare I set quite a high public  profile there I have many friends and  colleagues and my role now is one I'm  supporting Rachel with this palliative  care program the other is simply to  raise the morale of my many Ukrainian  friends and say we love you we care for  you we haven't forgotten you and that's  very important for them and have most of  the  doctors stayed behind or have a have a  good number left the country now  no they haven't left they haven't left  no no no they're all I mean because  they've been at War since 2014 with  Russia in fact the Ukrainian military is  relatively well set up  um and there are enough soldiers there's  enough doctors as far as I can make out  but there are of course an awful lot of  casualties and the casualties provenger  is produced by modern Munitions a  grotesque quite grotesque and Rachel how  are the hospitals I mean obviously  you're dealing with hospices but how you  must hear about the hospitals and the  doctors and the surgeons how are they  coping at the moment  well there's this  experience that we've heard an awful lot  about  um you know that there's a Defiance and  an absolute  seething hatred of Putin that uh you  know we were told about every single day  everyone I met was was seething towards  this man who's inflicting such Horrors  on their country  but really they the the doctors are  nurses they've got their backs against  the wall they have no alternative but to  keep on going because the only  alternative is a dictator who wants to  obliterate their country destroy their  country and what struck me with all the  doctors I spoke to was how very close to  the surface profound trauma was  everybody knows somebody who has been  murdered everybody's involved with  caring for victims and I have no doubt  that there is a silent epidemic of PTSD  post-traumatic stress disorder across  the country so doctors were gritting  their teeth doing what they can for each  other but my goodness the personal cost  for ukrainians is astonishing okay well  um Dr Clark Dr Marsh I appreciate you  both coming on the program thank you  very much indeed
 

